<h1>Bagua Chun Gen Qigong</h1>
<p style="font-family:'Cormorant Garamond',Georgia,serif;font-size:1.15em;color:#5A9EAD;" class="reveal">Eight exercises rooted in Taoist cosmology. Taught live, shaped entirely around you.</p>
<p style="margin-bottom:1.2em;" class="reveal"><a href="/why-qigong-training/" style="font-family:'Inter',sans-serif;font-size:0.82em;letter-spacing:1.5px;text-transform:uppercase;">→ Why choose this training?</a></p>

<div class="shared-story reveal">
<p>Bagua Chun Gen Qigong is a set of eight exercises that grew out of the Bagua, the eight trigrams at the heart of Taoist cosmology. One thing to clear up straight away: this is not Baguazhang. There is no circle walking here. Bagua Chun Gen is a completely different practice - a Qigong set that shares a philosophical root with the martial art but nothing of its method.</p>
</div>

<p class="reveal">What sets this form apart is its reach. It opens energy gates that rarely get a mention in mainstream teaching, and its movements work acupuncture points along all of the meridians. Few sets of this size cover so much ground, which is why it is such a versatile practice to own for life.</p>

<p class="reveal">The movements, though, are only the visible half of the work. Anyone can copy a shape from a video. What turns choreography into Qigong is what happens inside: the intention (Yi) that guides the Qi, the quality of attention that lets you actually feel what is happening, and the tangible feedback that builds your skill from one practice session to the next. That internal work is the core of this course, taught one to one by Seb.</p>

<h3 class="reveal">What You Will Learn</h3>
<ul class="features reveal">
<li>All eight exercises, taught movement by movement</li>
<li>The stance, posture and alignment that make the internal work possible</li>
<li>Which energy gates and acupuncture points each exercise addresses, and why</li>
<li>How to use breath and intention to guide the Qi, rather than merely picturing it</li>
<li>How to recognise and correct the faults that commonly creep into practice</li>
</ul>

<p class="reveal">By the end you will know not just the movements but which points and pathways each one focuses on.</p>

